WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.196 --> 00:00:01.669
- All right, before we say goodbye,

00:00:01.669 --> 00:00:03.120
we have time for one more story.

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:04.200
This is what I'm liking.

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:08.336
(rock music)
(audience cheering)

00:00:08.336 --> 00:00:11.580
The pandemic was hard on
everyone, especially teenage boys.

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:14.370
That's according to Chicago-based
clinical psychologist,

00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:15.780
Dr. John Duffy.

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:17.520
But he also says there's hope,

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:19.177
which he writes about in his new book,

00:00:19.177 --> 00:00:22.080
"Rescuing Our Sons: 8
Solutions to Our Crisis

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:23.910
of Disaffected Teen Boys."

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:25.710
Dr. Duffy is dialed in right now.

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:27.600
He watches on NBC Chicago.

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:29.090
What's up, Dr. Duffy?

00:00:29.090 --> 00:00:31.952
(audience clapping)
- Hi Kelly, how are you?

00:00:31.952 --> 00:00:32.785
- I'm so good.

00:00:32.785 --> 00:00:34.590
Thank you for joining us.

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:36.190
So, what changes have you noticed?

00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:38.400
- Okay, so since the pandemic,

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:40.950
teenage boys are telling me they are sad,

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:42.960
they're really quite lonely,

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:46.290
and they're disconnected from themselves

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:47.520
and from their emotions.

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:49.800
They don't really understand
their emotions very well

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:51.158
'cause we haven't taught them to do that.

00:00:51.158 --> 00:00:52.890
They don't understand the emotions

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:54.300
of the people around them.

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:56.340
We know they're disconnected
from us as parents

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:58.230
'cause they're up in that
room with the door closed

00:00:58.230 --> 00:01:01.590
or down in the basement
playing video games, right?

00:01:01.590 --> 00:01:04.110
And they're also
disconnected from their peers

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:07.320
because they really don't
have the great language

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:08.550
to speak to them.

00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:11.640
They talk via Snapchat
or video games with them.

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:14.160
So, the rich discussions
we want them to have,

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:16.745
they don't really have and
they don't really feel worthy

00:01:16.745 --> 00:01:19.230
of those good relationships.

00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:22.230
All this funnels into
a sense of hopelessness

00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:26.130
that has me concerned about
our teenage boys right now.

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:28.470
- Yeah, and what will
happen as they grow older

00:01:28.470 --> 00:01:29.760
if they don't have those skills.

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:31.410
So, what do you recommend for parents?

00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:33.621
- Yeah, so what parents tend to do

00:01:33.621 --> 00:01:38.010
is focus on those things
like the behaviors

00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:40.516
that our boys engage in that
don't work for them, right?

00:01:40.516 --> 00:01:44.700
If I could only get them to do X and Y

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:46.830
through a series of
punishments and consequences,

00:01:46.830 --> 00:01:48.570
then everything would be fine.

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:50.850
I want parents to abandon that for now

00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:53.640
and focus on your relationship with them

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:55.830
and the emotional bank
account that you have

00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:58.650
in that relationship and light up for them

00:01:58.650 --> 00:01:59.520
when you see them.

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:00.870
This is super important

00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:03.900
because so many boys tell me nobody ever

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:06.150
through the course of a day, month, year,

00:02:06.150 --> 00:02:08.070
lights up for them at all.

00:02:08.070 --> 00:02:09.865
So, use that emotional bank account,

00:02:09.865 --> 00:02:13.590
watch what he's watching, listen
to what he's listening to.

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:15.180
You can provide a counterpoint

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:17.280
to some of the negative vitriolic stuff

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:20.640
he's hearing on podcasts, on video games,

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:22.380
through YouTube videos.

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:24.825
It is a win-win-win for parenting, Kelly.

00:02:24.825 --> 00:02:26.970
Who wouldn't wanna be a part of that?

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:28.830
- Yeah, be involved is really,

00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:30.210
is really what it sounds like.

00:02:30.210 --> 00:02:33.870
Well, "Rescuing Our Sons:
8 Solutions to Our Crisis

00:02:33.870 --> 00:02:35.880
of Disaffected Teen Boys" is out now

00:02:35.880 --> 00:02:38.178
and everyone is going home with a copy.

00:02:38.178 --> 00:02:39.861
(audience cheering)

00:02:39.861 --> 00:02:41.721
Thank you so much, Dr. Duffy.

00:02:41.721 --> 00:02:44.613
I'm liking your post right now, all right.

00:02:44.613 --> 00:02:45.446
- [Dr. Duffy] Thank you, Kelly.

00:02:45.446 --> 00:02:46.680
- And that's not all.

00:02:46.680 --> 00:02:48.660
Our season long partner, Pilot Pen,

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:50.370
makers of Precise the go-to pen

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:51.390
of "The Kelly Clarkson Show"

00:02:51.390 --> 00:02:53.130
appreciate the work that you're doing.

00:02:53.130 --> 00:02:55.500
We mentioned the National
Alliance on Mental Illness

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:57.570
in the last segment, so on your behalf,

00:02:57.570 --> 00:03:00.134
Pilot will be writing
them a check for $1,000.

00:03:00.134 --> 00:03:02.130
(audience cheering)

00:03:02.130 --> 00:03:03.478
- Fantastic, thank you.

00:03:03.478 --> 00:03:05.689
- Thank you so much, Dr. Duffy.

00:03:05.689 --> 00:03:07.140
And thank you to all our guests this hour:

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:11.442
Dan Levy, Coral Pena,
Gesine Bullock-Prado,

00:03:11.442 --> 00:03:13.563
Wynonna Judd, and Trisha Yearwood.

